window.lectureSlides = [{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Theory\nDesign Theory\n1","title":"Design Theory","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Why normalization exists\nOne fact, one home: repeated facts create opportunities for disagreement.\nConstraints help: types, keys, and references reject many bad states.\nDesign finishes the job: decomposition removes the redundancy that constraints cannot.\nDesign Theory  >  Bridge  >  Why design matters\n2","title":"Why normalization exists","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Normal forms & functional dependencies\n3\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1","title":"1. Normal forms & functional dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nOverview of design theory & normal forms\nData anomalies & constraints\nFunctional dependencies\n4\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Theory\nDesign theory is about how to represent your data to avoid anomalies.\nIt is a mostly mechanical process\nTools can carry out routine portions\nWe have a notebook implementing all algorithms!\nWe’ll play with it in the activities!\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview","title":"Design Theory","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Normal Forms\n1st Normal Form (1NF) = All tables are flat\n2nd Normal Form = disused\nBoyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\n3rd Normal Form (3NF)\n4th and 5th Normal Forms = see text books\nDB designs based on functional dependencies, intended to prevent data anomalies\nOur focus in this lecture + next one\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview","title":"Normal Forms","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Theory\nDesign theory is about how to represent your data to avoid anomalies.\nIt is a mostly mechanical process\nTools can carry out routine portions\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview\n7","title":"Design Theory","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Normal Forms\n1st Normal Form (1NF) = All tables are flat\n2nd Normal Form\n3rd Normal Form (3NF)\nBoyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\n4th and 5th Normal Forms = see text books\nDB designs based on functional dependencies, intended to prevent data anomalies\nOur focus in this lecture + next one\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview\n8","title":"Normal Forms","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1st Normal Form (1NF)\n9\nStudent\nCourses\nMary\n{CS145,CS229}\nJoe\n{CS145,CS106}\n…\n…\nViolates 1NF.\n1NF Constraint: Types must be atomic!\nStudent\nCourses\nMary\nCS145\nMary\nCS229\nJoe\nCS145\nJoe\nCS106\nIn 1st NF\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview","title":"1","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1st Normal Form (1NF)\nViolates 1NF.\n1NF Constraint: Types must be atomic!\nIn 1st NF\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Overview\n10\nStudent\nCourses\nMary\n{CS145,CS229}\nJoe\n{CS145,CS106}\n…\n…\nStudent\nCourses\nMary\nCS145\nMary\nCS229\nJoe\nCS145\nJoe\nCS106","title":"1","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Data Anomalies & Constraints\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n11","title":"Data Anomalies & Constraints","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nIf every course is in only one room, contains redundant information!\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\n13\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS4450\nB01\nJoe\nCS4450\nB01\nSam\nCS4459\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nIf every course is in only one room, contains redundant information!\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\n14\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nC12\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nIf we update the room number for one tuple, we get inconsistent data = an update anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\n15\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\n..\n..\n..\nIf everyone drops the class, we lose what room the class is in! = a delete anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\n16\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nSimilarly, we can’t reserve a room without students = an insert anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\n…\nCS229\nC12\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\n17\nStudent\nCourse\nMary\nCS145\nJoe\nCS145\nSam\nCS145\n..\n..\nCourse\nRoom\nCS145\nB01\nCS229\nC12\nToday: develop theory to understand why this design may be  better and how to find this decomposition…\nIs this form better?\nRedundancy?\nUpdate anomaly?\nDelete anomaly?\nInsert anomaly?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nIf every course is in only one room, contains redundant information!\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n18\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS4450\nB01\nJoe\nCS4450\nB01\nSam\nCS4459\nB01\n..\n..\n..","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nIf we update the room number for one tuple, we get inconsistent data = an update anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n19\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nC12\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nIf everyone drops the class, we lose what room the class is in! = a delete anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n20\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\n..\n..\n..","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nSimilarly, we can’t reserve a room without students = an insert anomaly\nA poorly designed database causes anomalies:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n21\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\n…\nCS229\nC12","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints Prevent (some) Anomalies in the Data\nToday: develop theory to understand why this design may be  better and how to find this decomposition…\nIs this form better?\nRedundancy?\nUpdate anomaly?\nDelete anomaly?\nInsert anomaly?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Data anomalies & constraints\n22\nStudent\nCourse\nMary\nCS145\nJoe\nCS145\nSam\nCS145\n..\n..\nCourse\nRoom\nCS145\nB01\nCS229\nC12","title":"Constraints Prevent (some) ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Checkpoint: name the anomaly\nUpdate anomaly: one fact must be changed in several rows.\nInsert anomaly: a fact cannot be recorded until an unrelated fact exists.\nDelete anomaly: removing one fact accidentally erases another.\nTest yourself: which anomaly occurs when the last student drops a course?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Retrieval check\n23","title":"Checkpoint: name the anomaly","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependencies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n24","title":"Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependency\nA->B means that\n“whenever two tuples agree on A then they agree on B.”\nDef: Let A,B be sets of attributes\nWe write A  B or say A functionally determines B if, for any tuples t1 and t2:\nt1[A] = t2[A] implies t1[B] = t2[B]\nand we call A  B a functional dependency\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n25","title":"Functional Dependency","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\n26\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nA Picture Of FDs\n27\nti\ntj\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"   ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\n28\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nti[A1] = tj[A1] AND ti[A2]=tj[A2] AND … AND ti[Am] = tj[Am]\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nti\ntj\nIf t1,t2 agree here..\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nif ti[A1] = tj[A1] AND ti[A2]=tj[A2] AND … AND ti[Am] = tj[Am]\nthen ti[B1] = tj[B1] AND ti[B2]=tj[B2] AND … AND ti[Bn] = tj[Bn]\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nti\ntj\nIf t1,t2 agree here..\n…they also agree here!\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\n30\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nif ti[A1] = tj[A1] AND ti[A2]=tj[A2] AND … AND ti[Am] = tj[Am]\nthen ti[B1] = tj[B1] AND ti[B2]=tj[B2] AND … AND ti[Bn] = tj[Bn]\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nti\ntj\nIf t1,t2 agree here..\n…they also must agree here!\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n31\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\nti\ntj\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n32\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"   ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nti[A1] = tj[A1] AND ti[A2]=tj[A2] AND … AND ti[Am] = tj[Am]\nti\ntj\nIf t1,t2 agree here..\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n33\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture Of FDs\nDefn (again):\nGiven attribute sets A={A1,…,Am} and B = {B1,…Bn} in R,\nThe functional dependency A B on R holds if for any ti,tj in R:\nif ti[A1] = tj[A1] AND ti[A2]=tj[A2] AND … AND ti[Am] = tj[Am]\nthen ti[B1] = tj[B1] AND ti[B2]=tj[B2] AND … AND ti[Bn] = tj[Bn]\nti\ntj\nIf t1,t2 agree here..\n…they also must agree here!\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n34\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"A Picture Of FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"FDs for Relational Schema Design\nHigh-level idea: why do we care about FDs?\nStart with some relational schema\nFind out its functional dependencies (FDs)\nUse these to design a better schema\nOne which minimizes the possibility of anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies\n35","title":"FDs for Relational Schema Design","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints\n36\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nNote: The FD {Course} -> {Room} holds on this instance\nA functional dependency is a form of constraint\nHolds on some instances (but not others) – can check whether there are violations\nPart of the schema, helps define a valid instance\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1\nRecall: an instance of a schema is a multiset of tuples conforming to that schema, i.e. a table","title":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints\n37\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nHowever, cannot prove that the FD {Course} -> {Room} is part of the schema\nNote that:\nYou can check if an FD is violated by examining a single instance;\nHowever, you cannot prove that an FD is part of the schema by examining a single instance.\nThis would require checking every valid instance\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1","title":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n38\nAn FD is a constraint which holds, or does not hold on an instance:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n39\n{Position}   {Phone}\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876    \nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876    \nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n40\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234    \nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234    \nLawyer\nbut not {Phone}    {Position}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY\n41\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  ACTIVITY\nA\nB\nC\nD\nE\n1\n2\n4\n3\n6\n3\n2\n5\n1\n8\n1\n4\n4\n5\n7\n1\n2\n4\n3\n6\n3\n2\n5\n1\n8\nFind at least three FDs which are violated on this instance:\n{    }  {    }\n{    }  {    }\n{    }  {    }","title":"ACTIVITY","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints\nNote: The FD {Course} -> {Room} holds on this instance\nA functional dependency is a form of constraint\nHolds on some instances (but not others) – can check whether there are violations\nPart of the schema, helps define a valid instance\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1\nRecall: an instance of a schema is a multiset of tuples conforming to that schema, i.e. a table\n42\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..","title":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints\nHowever, cannot prove that the FD {Course} -> {Room} is part of the schema\nNote that:\nYou can check if an FD is violated by examining a single instance;\nHowever, you cannot prove that an FD is part of the schema by examining a single instance.\nThis would require checking every valid instance\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1\n43\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..","title":"Functional Dependencies as Constraints","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Evidence is not a business rule\nA sample can disprove an FD: one violating pair is enough.\nA sample cannot prove an FD: future valid rows may still violate the pattern.\nThe schema supplies the rule: derive FDs from domain meaning and declared constraints.\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  FD misconception\n44","title":"Evidence is not a business rule","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n45\nAn FD is a constraint which holds, or does not hold on an instance:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n46\n{Position}   {Phone}\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876    \nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876    \nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"More Examples\n47\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234    \nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234    \nLawyer\nbut not {Phone}    {Position}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  Functional dependencies","title":"More Examples","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY\n48\nDesign Theory  >  Section 1  >  ACTIVITY\nA\nB\nC\nD\nE\n1\n2\n4\n3\n6\n3\n2\n5\n1\n8\n1\n4\n4\n5\n7\n1\n2\n4\n3\n6\n3\n2\n5\n1\n8\nFind at least three FDs which are violated on this instance:\n{    }  {    }\n{    }  {    }\n{    }  {    }","title":"ACTIVITY","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2. Finding functional dependencies\n49\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2","title":"2. Finding functional dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\n“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs: Intuition\nFinding FDs\nClosures\nACTIVITY: Compute the closures\n50\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\n51\nWe can start to develop a notion of good vs. bad FDs:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nIntuitively:\nEmpID -> Name, Phone, Position is “good FD”\nMinimal redundancy, less possibility of anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs","title":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\n52\nWe can start to develop a notion of good vs. bad FDs:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nIntuitively:\nEmpID -> Name, Phone, Position is “good FD”\nBut Position -> Phone is a “bad FD”\nRedundancy!  Possibility of data anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs","title":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Student\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..\nGiven a set of FDs (from user) our goal is to:\nFind all FDs, and\nEliminate the “Bad Ones\".\nReturning to our original example… can you see how the “bad FD” {Course} -> {Room} could lead to an:\nUpdate Anomaly\nInsert Anomaly\nDelete Anomaly\n…\n“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\n53\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs","title":"Student","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\n54\nWe can start to develop a notion of good vs. bad FDs:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nIntuitively:\nEmpID -> Name, Phone, Position is “good FD”\nMinimal redundancy, less possibility of anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs","title":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\n55\nWe can start to develop a notion of good vs. bad FDs:\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nIntuitively:\nEmpID -> Name, Phone, Position is “good FD”\nBut Position -> Phone is a “bad FD”\nRedundancy!  Possibility of data anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs","title":"“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Given a set of FDs (from user) our goal is to:\nFind all FDs, and\nEliminate the “Bad Ones\".\nReturning to our original example… can you see how the “bad FD” {Course} -> {Room} could lead to an:\nUpdate Anomaly\nInsert Anomaly\nDelete Anomaly\n…\n“Good” vs. “Bad” FDs\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Good vs. Bad FDs\n56\nStudent\nCourse\nRoom\nMary\nCS145\nB01\nJoe\nCS145\nB01\nSam\nCS145\nB01\n..\n..\n..","title":"Student","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"FDs for Relational Schema Design\nHigh-level idea: why do we care about FDs?\nStart with some relational schema\nFind out its functional dependencies (FDs)\nUse these to design a better schema\nOne which minimizes possibility of anomalies\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\nThis part can be tricky!\n57","title":"FDs for Relational Schema Design","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\nThere can be a very large number of FDs…\nHow to find them all efficiently?\nWe can’t necessarily show that any FD will hold on all instances…\nHow to do this?\nWe will start with this problem:\nGiven a set of FDs, F, what other FDs must hold?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n58","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\nEquivalent to asking: Given a set of FDs, F = {f1,…fn}, does an FD g hold?\nInference problem: How do we decide?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n60\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Department}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nName\nColor\nCategory\nDep\nPrice\nGizmo\nGreen\nGadget\nToys\n49\nWidget\nBlack\nGadget\nToys\n59\nGizmo\nGreen\nWhatsit\nGarden\n99\nWhich / how many other FDs do?!?\nProvided FDs:\nProducts\nGiven the provided FDs, we can see that {Name, Category}  {Price} must also hold on any instance…\nExample:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Department}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nWhich / how many other FDs do?!?\nProvided FDs:\nProducts\nGiven the provided FDs, we can see that {Name, Category}  {Price} must also hold on any instance…\nExample:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n61\nName\nColor\nCategory\nDep\nPrice\nGizmo\nGreen\nGadget\nToys\n49\nWidget\nBlack\nGadget\nToys\n59\nGizmo\nGreen\nWhatsit\nGarden\n99","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\nEquivalent to asking: Given a set of FDs, F = {f1,…fn}, does an FD g hold?\nInference problem: How do we decide?\nAnswer: Three simple rules called Armstrong’s Rules.\nSplit/Combine,\nReduction, and\nTransitivity… ideas by picture\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n62","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\n63\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\n64\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\n… is equivalent to the following n FDs…\nA1,…,Am  Bi for i=1,…,n\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\n65\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\n… is equivalent to …\nAnd vice-versa, A1,…,Am  Bi for i=1,…,n\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Reduction/Trivial\nA1\n…\nAm\nA1,…,Am  Aj for any j=1,…,m\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Reduction/Trivial","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n67\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\n… is equivalent to the following n FDs…\nA1,…,Am  Bi for i=1,…,n\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n68\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Split/Combine\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn\n… is equivalent to …\nAnd vice-versa, A1,…,Am  Bi for i=1,…,n\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n69\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn","title":"1. Split/Combine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nName, Color -> price, Dept\nSplit:\nName, Color -> Price\nName, Color -> Dept\n70","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Reduction/Trivial\n71\nA1\n…\nAm\nA1,…,Am  Aj for any j=1,…,m\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\nReduction:\nName, Color -> Name\nName, Color -> Color","title":"Reduction/Trivial","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Reduction/Trivial\nA1,…,Am  Aj for any j=1,…,m\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n72\nReduction:\nName, Color -> Name\nName, Color -> Color\nA1\n…\nAm","title":"Reduction/Trivial","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nReduction:\nName, Color -> Name\nName, Color -> Color\n73","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Transitive Closure\n74\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nC1\n…\nCk\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn and\nB1,…,Bn  C1,…,Ck\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"3. Transitive Closure","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Transitive Closure\n75\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nC1\n…\nCk\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn and\nB1,…,Bn  C1,…,Ck\nimplies\nA1,…,Am  C1,…,Ck\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"3. Transitive Closure","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Transitive Closure\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn and\nB1,…,Bn  C1,…,Ck\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n76\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nC1\n…\nCk","title":"3. Transitive Closure","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Transitive Closure\nA1, …, Am  B1,…,Bn and\nB1,…,Bn  C1,…,Ck\nimplies\nA1,…,Am  C1,…,Ck\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n77\nA1\n…\nAm\nB1\n…\nBn\nC1\n…\nCk","title":"3. Transitive Closure","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n{Name} -> {color}\n{color, category} --> {price}\nThen,\n{Name, category} -> {price}\n78","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n79\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Department}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nName\nColor\nCategory\nDep\nPrice\nGizmo\nGreen\nGadget\nToys\n49\nWidget\nBlack\nGadget\nToys\n59\nGizmo\nGreen\nWhatsit\nGarden\n99\nWhich / how many other FDs hold?\nProvided FDs:\nProducts\nExample:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nWhich / how many other FDs hold?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\n?\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\n?\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\n?\n7. {Name, Category -> {Color, Category}\n?\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\n?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nCan we find an algorithmic way to do this?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\nTrivial\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\nTransitive (4 -> 1)\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\nTrivial\n7. {Name, Category -> {Color, Category}\nSplit/combine (5 + 6)\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\nTransitive (7 -> 3)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n82\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nWhich / how many other FDs hold?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\n?\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\n?\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\n?\n7. {Name, Category} -> {Color, Category}\n?\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\n?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n83\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nCan we find an algorithmic way to do this?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\nTrivial\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\nTransitive (4 -> 1)\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\nTrivial\n7. {Name, Category} -> {Color, Category}\nSplit/combine (5 + 6)\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\nTransitive (7 -> 3)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Department}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nWhich / how many other FDs hold?\nProvided FDs:\nProducts\nExample:\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n84\nName\nColor\nCategory\nDep\nPrice\nGizmo\nGreen\nGadget\nToys\n49\nWidget\nBlack\nGadget\nToys\n59\nGizmo\nGreen\nWhatsit\nGarden\n99","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nWhich / how many other FDs hold?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\n?\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\n?\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\n?\n7. {Name, Category} -> {Color, Category}\n?\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\n?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n85","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Functional Dependencies\n1. {Name}  {Color}\n2. {Category}  {Dept.}\n3. {Color, Category}  {Price}\nCan we find an algorithmic way to do this?\nProvided FDs:\nInferred FDs:\nExample:\nInferred FD\nRule used\n4. {Name, Category} -> {Name}\nTrivial\n5. {Name, Category} -> {Color}\nTransitive (4 -> 1)\n6. {Name, Category} -> {Category}\nTrivial\n7. {Name, Category} -> {Color, Category}\nSplit/combine (5 + 6)\n8. {Name, Category} -> {Price}\nTransitive (7 -> 3)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Finding FDs\n86","title":"Finding Functional Dependencies","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A repeatable FD inference routine\nNormalize the goal: split a multi-attribute right side into one attribute at a time.\nExpand what is known: apply FDs whose left side is already available.\nStop at a fixed point: no new attribute means the closure is complete.\nExplain the proof: record which FD added each attribute.\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Strategy\n87","title":"A repeatable FD inference routine","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closures\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures\n88","title":"Closures","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure of a set of Attributes\n89\nGiven a set of attributes  A1, …, An and a set of FDs F:\nThen the closure, {A1, …, An}+ is the set of attributes B s.t. {A1, …, An}  B\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {department}\n{color, category}  {price}\nExample:      F =\nExample Closures:\n{name}+ = {name, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept, price}\n{color}+ = {color}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure of a set of Attributes","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n90\nStart with X = {A1, …, An} and set of FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is entailed by F\nand {B1, …, Bn}  X\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n91\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n92\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n93\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n94\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept, price}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n95\nCompute {A,B}+ = {A, B,                             }\nCompute {A, F}+ = {A, F,                             }\nR(A,B,C,D,E,F)\n{A,B}  {C}\n{A,D}  {E}\n{B}  {D}\n{A,F}  {B}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n96\nCompute {A,B}+ = {A, B, C, D                          }\nCompute {A, F}+ = {A, F, B                            }\nR(A,B,C,D,E,F)\n{A,B}  {C}\n{A,D}  {E}\n{B}  {D}\n{A,F}  {B}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n97\nCompute {A,B}+ = {A, B, C, D, E}\nCompute {A, F}+ = {A, B, C, D, E, F}\nR(A,B,C,D,E,F)\n{A,B}  {C}\n{A,D}  {E}\n{B}  {D}\n{A,F}  {B}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure of a set of Attributes\n98\nGiven a set of attributes  A1, …, An and a set of FDs F:\nThen the closure, {A1, …, An}+ is the set of attributes B s.t. {A1, …, An}  B\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nExample:      F =\nExample Closures:\n{name}+ = {name, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept, price}\n{color}+ = {color}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure of a set of Attributes","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n99\nStart with X = {A1, …, An} and set of FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is entailed by F\nand {B1, …, Bn}  X\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n100\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n101\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n102\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n103\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept, price}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n104\nStart with X = {A1, …, An} and set of FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is entailed by F\nand {B1, …, Bn}  X\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n105\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n106\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n107\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Closure Algorithm\n108\nStart with X = {A1, …, An}, FDs F.\nRepeat until X doesn’t change; do:\nif  {B1, …, Bn}  C is in F and {B1, …, Bn}  X:\nthen  add C to X.\nReturn X as X+\nF =\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept, price}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color}\n{name, category}+ =\n{name, category, color, dept}\n{name}  {color}\n{category}  {dept}\n{color, category}  {price}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Closure Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n109\nCompute {A,B}+ = {A, B,                             }\nCompute {A, F}+ = {A, F,                             }\nCompute {A, C}+ = {                             }\nR(A,B,C,D,E,F, H)\n{A,B}  {C}\n{A,D}  {E}\n{B}  {D}\n{F}  {H}\n{A,F}  {B}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 2  >  Closures","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Closures, Superkeys & Keys\n110\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3","title":"3. Closures, ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nClosures Pt. II\nSuperkeys & Keys\nACTIVITY:  The key or a key?\n111\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Why Do We Need the Closure?\nWith closure we can find all FD’s easily\nTo check if X ® A\nCompute X+\nCheck if A Î X+\n112\nNote here that X is a set of attributes, but A is a single attribute.  Why does considering FDs of this form suffice?\nRecall the Split/combine rule:\nX  A1, …, X  An\nimplies\nX  {A1, …, An}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II","title":"Why Do We Need the Closure?","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs\n113\n{A,B}  C{A,D}  B\n{B}    D\nExample:\nGiven F =\nStep 1: Compute X+, for every set of attributes X:\n{A}+ = {A}\n{B}+ = {B,D}\n{C}+ = {C}\n{D}+ = {D}\n{A,B}+ = {A,B,C,D}\n{A,C}+ = {A,C}\n{A,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\n{A,B,C}+ = {A,B,D}+ = {A,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D} {B,C,D}+ = {B,C,D}\n{A,B,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\nNo need to compute all of these- why?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II","title":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs\n114\n{A,B}  C{A,D}  B\n{B}    D\nExample:\nGiven F =\nFor table R(A, B, C, D)\nStep 1: Compute X+, for every set of attributes X:\n{A}+ = {A}\n{B}+ = {B,D}\n{C}+ = {C}\n{D}+ = {D}\n{A,B}+ = {A,B,C,D}\n{A,C}+ = {A,C}\n{A,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\n{B,C} + = {B,C,D}\n{B,D} + = {B,D}\n{C,D} + = {C,D}\n{A,B,C}+ = {A,B,D}+ = {A,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\n{B,C,D}+ = {B,C,D}\n{A,B,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\nNo need to compute all of these- why?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II","title":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs\n115\n{A,B}  C{A,D}  B\n{B}    D\nExample:\nGiven F =\nStep 1: Compute X+, for every set of attributes X:\n{A}+ = {A}, {B}+ = {B,D}, {C}+ = {C}, {D}+ = {D}, {A,B}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,C}+ = {A,C}, {A,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,B,C}+ = {A,B,D}+ = {A,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {B,C,D}+ = {B,C,D},    {A,B,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\nStep 2: Enumerate all FDs X  Y, s.t. Y  X+ and X  Y = :\n{A,B}  {C,D}, {A,D}  {B,C},\n{A,B,C}  {D}, {A,B,D}  {C},\n{A,C,D}  {B}\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II","title":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs\n116\n{A,B}  C{A,D}  B\n{B}    D\nExample:\nGiven F =\n{A}+ = {A}, {B}+ = {B,D}, {C}+ = {C}, {D}+ = {D}, {A,B}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,C}+ = {A,C}, {A,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,B,C}+ = {A,B,D}+ = {A,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {B,C,D}+ = {B,C,D},    {A,B,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\nStep 2: Enumerate all FDs X  Y, s.t. Y  X+ and X  Y = :\n{A,B}  {C,D}, {A,D}  {B,C},\n{A,B,C}  {D}, {A,B,D}  {C},\n{A,C,D}  {B}\n“Y is in the closure of X”\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II\nStep 1: Compute X+, for every set of attributes X:","title":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs\n117\n{A,B}  C{A,D}  B\n{B}    D\nExample:\nGiven F =\n{A}+ = {A}, {B}+ = {B,D}, {C}+ = {C}, {D}+ = {D}, {A,B}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,C}+ = {A,C}, {A,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {A,B,C}+ = {A,B,D}+ = {A,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}, {B,C,D}+ = {B,C,D},    {A,B,C,D}+ = {A,B,C,D}\nStep 2: Enumerate all FDs X  Y, s.t. Y  X+ and X  Y = :\n{A,B}  {C,D}, {A,D}  {B,C},\n{A,B,C}  {D}, {A,B,D}  {C},\n{A,C,D}  {B}\nThe FD X  Y is non-trivial\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Closures Pt. II\nStep 1: Compute X+, for every set of attributes X:","title":"Using Closure to Infer ALL FDs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Fast path to candidate keys\nStart with must-have attributes: attributes never appearing on an FD's right side belong in every key.\nAdd only what is needed: compute closure after each addition.\nProve minimality: remove each attribute once; the closure must stop covering the schema.\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Key strategy\n118","title":"Fast path to candidate keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Superkeys and Keys\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\n119","title":"Superkeys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys and Superkeys\nA superkey is a set of attributes A1, …, An s.t.\nfor any other attribute B in R,\nwe have  {A1, …, An}  B\nA key is a minimal superkey\nI.e. all attributes are functionally determined by a superkey\nThis means that no subset of a key is also a superkey (i.e., dropping any attribute from the key makes it no longer a superkey)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys","title":"Keys and ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys and Superkeys\n121\nA superkey is a set of attributes A1, …, An s.t.\nfor any other attribute B in R,\nwe have  {A1, …, An}  B\nA key is a minimal superkey\nI.e. all attributes are functionally determined by a superkey\nThis means that no subset of a key is also a superkey (i.e., dropping any attribute from the key makes it no longer a superkey)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\ni.e.,","title":"Keys and ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys and Superkeys\nA superkey is a set of attributes A1, …, An s.t.\nfor any other attribute B in R,\nwe have  {A1, …, An}  B\nA key is a minimal superkey\nI.e. all attributes are functionally determined by a superkey\nThis means that no subset of a key is also a superkey (i.e., dropping any attribute from the key makes it no longer a superkey)\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\n122\ni.e.,","title":"Keys and ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Finding Keys and Superkeys\nFor each set of attributes X\nCompute X+\nIf X+ = set of all attributes then X is a superkey\nIf X is minimal, then it is a key\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\n123","title":"Finding Keys and ","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example of Finding Keys\nProduct(name, price, category, color)\n{name, category}  price\n{category}  color\nWhat is a key?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys","title":"Example of Finding Keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example of Keys\nProduct(name, price, category, color)\n{name, category}  price\n{category}  color\n{name, category}+ = {name, price, category, color}\n= the set of all attributes\nthis is a superkey\nthis is a key, since neither name nor category alone is a superkey\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys","title":"Example of Keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example of Finding Keys\nProduct(name, price, category, color)\n{name, category}  price\n{category}  color\nWhat is a key?\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\n126\n{name, category}+ = {name, category, color, price}\n{name, category, color}+ = {name, category, color, price}","title":"Example of Finding Keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example of Keys\n127\nProduct(name, price, category, color)\n{name, category}  price\n{category}  color\n{name, category}+ = {name, price, category, color}\n= the set of all attributes\nthis is a superkey\nthis is a key, since neither name nor category alone is a superkey\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys","title":"Example of Keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example of Keys\nProduct(name, price, category, color)\n{name, category}  price\n{category}  color\n{name, category}+ = {name, price, category, color}\n= the set of all attributes\nthis is a superkey\nthis is a key, since neither name nor category alone is a superkey\nDesign Theory  >  Section 3  >  Superkeys & Keys\n128","title":"Example of Keys","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Superkey vs Key\n{name, category}+ = {name, category, color, price}\nsuperkey and key\n{name, category, color}+ = {name, category, color, price}\nSuperkey but not key\n129","title":"Superkey","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Theory II\nDesign Theory II\n130","title":"Design Theory II","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Normal Forms\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Normal Forms","title":"Normal Forms","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Design\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Conceptual Design","title":"Conceptual Design","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Definitions for Normal Forms\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Normal Forms","title":"Definitions for Normal Forms","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nLOTS(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate)\nFD1: p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate}\nFD2: county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price, tax_rate}\nFD3:  county_name -> Tax_rate\nFD4: area -> price\n(p_id)+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name, lot#}+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name}+ ={county_name, tax_rate}\n{area}+={price, area}\nPrime attributes:\np_id\ncounty_name\nlot#\nnon-prime attributes:\nArea, Price, Tax rate\nIt is not in 2NF as we have county_name -> tax_rate\nLots1( p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nLots2(county_name, tax_rate)\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Normal Forms","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF -> 3NF\n3NF\nFD: X->A\n(a) X is a superkey\nor (b) A is a prime attribute\nLots1(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nFD1:p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price}\nFD2:{county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price}\nFD4:area -> price\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nLots1B(area, price)\n2NF\n3NF\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Normal Forms","title":"2NF -> 3NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3NF to BCNF\n3NF\nBCNF\nBCNF:\nX->A\nX has to be a superkey\nFD5:\nArea- > county_name\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nis in 3NF but not in BCNF (county is prime attribute)\nLots1AX(p_id,  lot#, Area)\nLots1AY(Area,county_name )\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 1  >  Normal Forms","title":"3NF to BCNF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Boyce-Codd Normal Form\n137\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2","title":"1. Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Back to Conceptual Design\nNow that we know how to find FDs, it’s a straight-forward process:\nSearch for “bad” FDs\nIf there are any, then keep decomposing the table into sub-tables until no more bad FDs\nWhen done, the database schema is normalized\n138\nRecall: there are several normal forms…\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  Conceptual Design","title":"Back to Conceptual Design","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\nMain idea is that we define “good” and “bad” FDs as follows:\nX  A is a “good FD” if X is a (super)key\nIn other words, if A is the set of all attributes\nX  A is a “bad FD” otherwise\nWe will try to eliminate the “bad” FDs!\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\nWhy does this definition of “good” and “bad” FDs make sense?\nIf X is not a (super)key, it functionally determines some of the attributes; therefore, those other attributes can be duplicated\nRecall: this means there is redundancy\nAnd redundancy like this can lead to data anomalies!\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form\n141\nBCNF is a simple condition for removing anomalies from relations:\nIn other words: there are no “bad” FDs\nA relation R is in BCNF if:\nif {A1, ..., An}  B is a non-trivial FD in R\nthen {A1, ..., An}  is a superkey for R\nEquivalently:   sets of attributes X, either (X+ = X) or (X+ = all attributes)\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n142\nWhat is the key?\n{SSN, PhoneNumber}\nName\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\nSeattle\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\nWestfield\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nWestfield\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNot in BCNF\nThis FD is bad because it is not a superkey\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2 >  BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n143\nName\nSSN\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\nMadison\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nLet’s check anomalies:\nRedundancy ?\nUpdate ?\nDelete ?\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNow in BCNF!\nThis FD is now good because it is the key\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n144\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n145\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nFind a set of attributes X which has non-trivial “bad” FDs, i.e. is not a superkey, using closures\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n146\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nIf no “bad” FDs found, in BCNF!\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n147\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nLet Y be the attributes that X functionally determines (+ that are not in X)\nAnd let Z be the complement, the other attributes that it doesn’t\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n148\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nX\nZ\nY\nR1\nR2\nSplit into one relation (table) with X plus the attributes that X determines (Y)…\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n149\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nX\nZ\nY\nR1\nR2\nAnd one relation with X plus the attributes it does not determine (Z)\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n150\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nProceed recursively until no more “bad” FDs!\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"R(A,B,C,D,E)\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nExample\n{A}  {B,C}\n{C}  {D}\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"R(A,B,C,D,E)","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n152\nR(A,B,C,D,E) {A}+ = {A,B,C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D,E}\nR1(A,B,C,D) {C}+ = {C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D}\nR2(A,E)\nR11(C,D)\nR12(A,B,C)\nR(A,B,C,D,E)\n{A}  {B,C}\n{C}  {D}\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2  >  BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Normal Forms\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms\n153","title":"Normal Forms","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Normal forms answer different questions\n2NF asks: does a non-prime attribute depend on only part of a composite key?\n3NF asks: does a non-key determinant create a transitive dependency?\nBCNF asks: is every non-trivial determinant a superkey?\nUse the ladder: identify the violating FD before choosing a decomposition.\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms  >  Decision ladder\n154","title":"Normal forms answer different questions","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF\nDefinition\nA relation schema R is in Second Normal Form (2NF) if every non-prime attribute A in R is not partially dependent on any key of R.\nPrime attribute:\nIs an attribute that is part of any key\nPartially dependent  depends on subset of the key\n155","title":"2NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nLOTS(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate)\nFD1: p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate}\nFD2: county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price, tax_rate}\nFD3:  county_name -> Tax_rate\nFD4: area -> price\n(p_id)+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name, lot#}+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name}+ ={county_name, tax_rate}\n{area}+={price, area}\nPrime attributes:\np_id\ncounty_name\nlot#\nnon-prime attributes:\nArea, Price, Tax rate\n156\nIt is not in 2NF as we have county_name -> tax_rate\nLots1( p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nLots2(county_name, tax_rate)\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms\nA relation schema R is in Second Normal Form (2NF) if every non-prime attribute A in R is not partially dependent on any key of R\nNon-prime attributes\nArea\nPid->Area                   (OK as PID is a key)\nCounty_name, lot#-> Area (OK as county_name, lot#  is a key\nPrice\nPid->Price\nCounty_name, lot# -- > price\nArea->price  (Area is not a key, OK)\nTax_rate\nPid-> tax_rate\nCounty_name, lot#-> tax_rate\nCounty_name-> tax_rate violates 2NF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF\n157\n2NF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms","title":"2NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nLOTS(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate)\nFD1: p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price, tax_rate}\nFD2: county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price, tax_rate}\nFD3:  county_name -> Tax_rate\nFD4: area -> price\n(p_id)+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name, lot#}+={ p_id, county_name, lot#, area, price, tax_rate}\n{county_name}+ ={county_name, tax_rate}\n{area}+={price, area}\nPrime attributes:\np_id\ncounty_name\nlot#\nnon-prime attributes:\nArea, Price, Tax rate\nIt is not in 2NF as we have county_name -> tax_rate\nLots1( p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nLots2(county_name, tax_rate)\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms\n158\nA relation schema R is in Second Normal Form (2NF) if every non-prime attribute A in R is not partially dependent on any key of R\nNon-prime attributes\nArea\nPid->Area                   (OK as PID is a key)\nCounty_name, lot#-> Area (OK as county_name, lot#  is a key\nPrice\nPid->Price\nCounty_name, lot# -- > price\nArea->price  (Area is not a key, OK)\nTax_rate\nPid-> tax_rate\nCounty_name, lot#-> tax_rate\nCounty_name-> tax_rate violates 2NF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF\n159\n2NF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms","title":"2NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3NF\nDefinition:\nA relation schema R is in Third Normal Form (3NF) if, whenever a nontrivial functional dependency X → A holds in R, either:\n(a) X is a superkey of R, or\n(b) A is a prime attribute of R.\n160","title":"3NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF -> 3NF\n161\nFD: X->A\n(a) X is a superkey\nor (b) A is a prime attribute\nLots1(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nFD1:p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price}\nFD2:{county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price}\nFD4:area -> price\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nLots1B(area, price)\n+ LOTS2\n2NF\n3NF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms","title":"2NF -> 3NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2NF -> 3NF\nFD: X->A\n(a) X is a superkey\nor (b) A is a prime attribute\nLots1(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area, price)\nFD1:p_id -> {county_name, lot#, Area, price}\nFD2:{county_name, lot#}->{p_id, area, price}\nFD4:area -> price\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nLots1B(area, price)\n+ LOTS2\n2NF\n3NF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms\n162","title":"2NF -> 3NF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"163\nBCNF:\nA relation schema R is in BCNF if, whenever a nontrivial functional dependency X → A holds in R, then\t X is a superkey of R\n3NF:\nA relation schema R is in Third Normal Form (3NF) if, whenever a nontrivial functional dependency X → A holds in R, either:\n(a) X is a superkey of R, or\n(b) A is a prime attribute of R.","title":"72","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF\nDefinition:\nA relation schema R is in BCNF if, whenever a nontrivial functional dependency X → A holds in R, then\t X is a superkey of R\n164","title":"BCNF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3NF to BCNF\nBCNF:\nX->A\nX has to be a superkey\nFD5:\nArea- > county_name\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nis in 3NF but not in BCNF (county is prime attribute)\nLots1AX(p_id,  lot#, Area)\nLots1AY(Area,county_name )\n165\n3NF\nBCNF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms","title":"3NF to BCNF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3NF to BCNF\n3NF\nBCNF\nDesign Theory II  >  Normal Forms\n166\nBCNF:\nX->A\nX has to be a superkey\nFD5:\nArea- > county_name\nLots1A(p_id, county_name, lot#, Area)\nis in 3NF but not in BCNF (county is prime attribute)\nLots1AX(p_id,  lot#, Area)\nLots1AY(Area,county_name )","title":"3NF to BCNF","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\nMain idea is that we define “good” and “bad” FDs as follows:\nX  A is a “good FD” if X is a (super)key\nIn other words, if A is the set of all attributes\nX  A is a “bad FD” otherwise\nWe will try to eliminate the “bad” FDs!\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF\n167","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\nWhy does this definition of “good” and “bad” FDs make sense?\nIf X is not a (super)key, it functionally determines some of the attributes; therefore, those other attributes can be duplicated\nRecall: this means there is redundancy\nAnd redundancy like this can lead to data anomalies!\n168\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form\n169\nBCNF is a simple condition for removing anomalies from relations:\nIn other words: there are no “bad” FDs\nA relation R is in BCNF if:\nif {A1, ..., An}  B is a non-trivial FD in R\nthen {A1, ..., An}  is a superkey for R\nEquivalently:   sets of attributes X, either (X+ = X) or (X+ = all attributes)\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n170\nWhat is the key?\n{SSN, PhoneNumber}\nName\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\nSeattle\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\nWestfield\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nWestfield\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNot in BCNF\nThis FD is bad because it is not a superkey\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2 >  BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n171\nName\nSSN\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\nMadison\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nLet’s check anomalies:\nRedundancy ?\nUpdate ?\nDelete ?\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNow in BCNF!\nThis FD is now good because it is the key\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\nWhy does this definition of “good” and “bad” FDs make sense?\nIf X is not a (super)key, it functionally determines some of the attributes; therefore, those other attributes can be duplicated\nRecall: this means there is redundancy\nAnd redundancy like this can lead to data anomalies!\nEmpID\nName\nPhone\nPosition\nE0045\nSmith\n1234\nClerk\nE3542\nMike\n9876\nSalesrep\nE1111\nSmith\n9876\nSalesrep\nE9999\nMary\n1234\nLawyer\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF\n172","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Boyce-Codd Normal Form\n173\nBCNF is a simple condition for removing anomalies from relations:\nIn other words: there are no “bad” FDs\nA relation R is in BCNF if:\nif {A1, ..., An}  B is a non-trivial FD in R\nthen {A1, ..., An}  is a superkey for R\nEquivalently:   sets of attributes X, either (X+ = X) or (X+ = all attributes)\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Boyce-","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n174\nWhat is the key?\n{SSN, PhoneNumber}\nName\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\nSeattle\nFred\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\nWestfield\nJoe\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nWestfield\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNot in BCNF\nThis FD is bad because it is not a superkey\nDesign Theory II  >  Section 2 >  BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n175\nName\nSSN\nCity\nFred\n123-45-6789\nSeattle\nJoe\n987-65-4321\nMadison\nSSN\nPhoneNumber\n123-45-6789\n206-555-1234\n123-45-6789\n206-555-6543\n987-65-4321\n908-555-2121\n987-65-4321\n908-555-1234\nLet’s check anomalies:\nRedundancy ?\nUpdate ?\nDelete ?\n{SSN}  {Name,City}\nNow in BCNF!\nThis FD is now good because it is the key\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n176\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n177\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nFind a set of attributes X which has non-trivial “bad” FDs, i.e. is not a superkey, using closures\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n178\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nIf no “bad” FDs found, in BCNF!\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n179\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nLet Y be the attributes that X functionally determines (+ that are not in X)\nAnd let Z be the complement, the other attributes that it doesn’t\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n180\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nX\nZ\nY\nR1\nR2\nSplit into one relation (table) with X plus the attributes that X determines (Y)…\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n181\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nX\nZ\nY\nR1\nR2\nAnd one relation with X plus the attributes it does not determine (Z)\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm\n182\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nProceed recursively until no more “bad” FDs!\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"BCNF Decomposition Algorithm","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"R(A,B,C,D,E)\nBCNFDecomp(R):   Find a set of attributes X s.t.: X+ ≠ X and X+ ≠ [all attributes]\nif (not found) then Return R\nlet Y = X+ - X,  Z = (X+)C    decompose R into R1(X  Y) and R2(X  Z)\nReturn BCNFDecomp(R1), BCNFDecomp(R2)\nExample\n{A}  {B,C}\n{C}  {D}\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF\n183","title":"R(A,B,C,D,E)","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n184\nR(A,B,C,D,E) {A}+ = {A,B,C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D,E}\nR1(A,B,C,D) {C}+ = {C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D}\nR2(A,E)\nR11(C,D)\nR12(A,B,C)\nR(A,B,C,D,E)\n{A}  {B,C}\n{C}  {D}\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lossless join decomposition\nDefinition:\nLossless Join Decomposition divides a relation R into smaller relations and\nGuarantees no data loss by allowing the original relation to be restored from the join of and\nAlso called non-additive decomposition.\nCondition for Lossless Join:\nThe decomposition is lossless if the natural join restores the original relation:\n185","title":"Lossless join decomposition","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n186\nR(A,B,C,D,E) {A}+ = {A,B,C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D,E}\nR1(A,B,C,D) {C}+ = {C,D} ≠ {A,B,C,D}\nR2(A,E)\nR11(C,D)\nR12(A,B,C)\nR(A,B,C,D,E)\n{A}  {B,C}\n{C}  {D}\nDesign Theory II  >   BCNF","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Two checks after decomposition\nLossless join: joining the pieces reconstructs exactly the original valid relation.\nDependency preservation: the original FDs can be enforced without joining the pieces.\nThey are different goals: BCNF guarantees a lossless step, but may sacrifice dependency preservation.\nDesign Theory II  >  BCNF  >  Quality checks\n187","title":"Two checks after decomposition","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lossless join decomposition\n188\nDefinition:\nLossless Join Decomposition divides a relation R into smaller relations and\nGuarantees no data loss by allowing the original relation to be restored from the join of and\nAlso called non-additive decomposition.\nCondition for Lossless Join:\nThe decomposition is lossless if the natural join restores the original relation:","title":"Lossless join decomposition","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lossless join decomposition\nCriteria for Lossless Join:\nA decomposition R=R1∪R2 ​ is lossless with respect to functional dependencies F if one of the following holds:\n(R1​∩R2​)→(R1​∖R2​)∈F+\n(R1​∩R2​)→(R2​∖R1​)∈F+\n189","title":"Lossless join decomposition","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Summary\nConstraints allow one to reason about redundancy in the data\nNormal forms describe how to remove this redundancy by decomposing relations\nElegant—by representing data appropriately certain errors are essentially impossible\nFor FDs, BCNF is the normal form.\nA tradeoff for insert performance: 3NF\nSUMMARY\n190","title":"Summary","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Exercise\nFind all candidate keys.\nIdentify the normal forms that R satisfy (1NF, 2NF, 3NF,BCNF).\nIf the relation is not in BNCF, decompose it until it becomes BCNF.\nAt each step, identify a new relation, decompose and re-compute the keys and the normal forms they satisfy.\nQuestion 1\nConsider relation: R = (A, B  , C, D, E) with the set of functional dependencies: {CE → D, D → B, C → A}\nQuestion 2\nConsider relation: R = (A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J)\nwith the set of functional dependencies: {AB → C, A → DE, B → F, F → GH, D → IJ}\nQuestion 3\nConsider relation: R = {A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J}\nand the set of functional dependencies: {AB → C, BD → EF, AD → GH, A → I, H → J}\n191","title":"Exercise","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Exit ticket: diagnose, prove, decompose\nDiagnose: identify one redundancy and the FD that causes it.\nProve: compute a closure and justify a candidate key.\nDecompose: name the violated normal form and split the relation.\nVerify: check losslessness and note whether dependencies are preserved.\nDesign Theory II  >  Close  >  Retrieval practice\n192","title":"Exit ticket: diagnose, prove, decompose","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lab: repair a flat enrollment table\nScenario: Enrollment(StudentID, StudentName, Major, CourseID, CourseTitle, InstructorID, InstructorName, Grade).\nAssume these FDs: StudentID -> StudentName, Major; CourseID -> CourseTitle, InstructorID; InstructorID -> InstructorName; StudentID, CourseID -> Grade.\nFirst diagnosis: identify one update, insertion, and deletion anomaly in the flat table.\nKey question: compute {StudentID, CourseID}+ and prove whether it is a candidate key.\nDesign Theory II  >  Lab  >  Scenario and evidence\n193","title":"Lab: repair a flat enrollment table","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decompose and verify\n1. Diagnose: list the anomalies and connect each to a responsible FD.\n2. Decompose: produce relations in 3NF or BCNF; label every primary and foreign key.\n3. Verify: explain why the join is lossless and whether the listed FDs are preserved.\n4. Test: insert two enrollments, update one instructor name, and delete one enrollment without losing course or student facts.\nDesign Theory II  >  Lab  >  Submission checklist\n194","title":"Decompose and verify","width":1440,"height":810,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"195\nPractice and hosted labs\nDependencies, keys and normalization\nNormal forms and normalization exercises\nForeign-key checks after decomposition\nYour task: Compute an attribute closure, identify a candidate key, decompose a relation, and justify losslessness and dependency preservation.\nHosted on GitHub Pages. Internet access required.","title":"Practice and hosted labs","width":1440,"height":810,"links":["https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/normalization/normal-forms.html","https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/joins-foreign-keys/foreign-keys.html"]}];
